  description: 'Product ownership, testing, security, platform engineering: most of what got hired as a separate seat over the last ten years is a skill that never needed one. On my team the fix landed on the tech lead, who now carries product judgment next to the domain expertise he already had. One field report, not a template — and it updates something I only got half right two posts ago.',
  html: `<div class="post-tldr"><p><strong>TL;DR</strong> <abbr title="Product Owner — the Scrum role responsible for the backlog">PO</abbr>, <abbr title="Quality Assurance">QA</abbr>, security, <abbr title="Platform engineering and operations, often shortened to DevOps">DevOps</abbr>, and plenty of others: job titles that barely existed twenty years ago, now hired and promoted against as if they always had. Each names a real skill. None ever required a seat that could only do that one thing. On my team, the fix landed on the tech lead, who absorbed product ownership on top of domain expertise he already carried — one field report, not a template for every org. The general claim underneath it: heading into 2027, an <abbr title="Built around AI agents doing first-draft, first-pass work by default, not as an add-on">AI-native</abbr> team can no longer afford the coordination a seat was built to sell, and skills have to remerge into whoever already holds the accountability. This sharpens something I only got half right in an earlier post — I said it plainly there, I'll say it plainer here.</p></div>
<hr>
<p>Somewhere in the last decade, a set of skills got promoted into a payroll line.</p>
<p>Product Owner. QA engineer. Security engineer. DevOps engineer. And plenty of others. Job titles that did not exist as separate seats twenty years ago, and that most engineering organizations now hire, budget and promote against as if they always had. Each one names something real: product judgment, test judgment, security judgment, operational judgment. None of them ever required a person who could only do that one thing. Development and architecture went through a version of the same move at some organizations — a Solution Architect title peeled off the senior developer who used to just hold both — but that pairing mostly resisted the split. A tech lead, by default, is already the person who carries the development judgment and the architecture judgment; that was never the piece that needed its own seat. Somewhere between the first Scrum certification and the fortieth dedicated Slack channel, the other skills got seats instead, and the seats started acting like they needed protecting from the rest of the team.</p>
<p>This is a field report about what happens when you put the seat back where the skill came from. It worked on my team. I do not think the specific shape it took there — the tech lead absorbing product ownership and doubling down on domain expertise — is the shape it should take everywhere; a security-heavy fintech team would land somewhere else, and should. What I think generalizes, heading into 2027, is the diagnosis underneath it: the seat, not the skill, is what a fast organization can no longer afford, and if you are running on <abbr title="Built around AI agents doing first-draft, first-pass work by default, not as an add-on">AI-native</abbr> throughput, the org chart you inherited is now the slowest thing you own.</p>
<blockquote><p>A title bought coordination. It rarely bought the judgment it was named after.</p></blockquote>

<h2>What the decade actually built</h2>
<p>I want to be honest about the history before I argue with it, because the cynical version is also the lazy one. Three things happened at once, and only one of them deserves the accusation.</p>
<p>The first is real: complexity grew faster than any one engineer could hold. Security surface, cloud topology, regulatory traceability — each got deep enough that a generalist skimming all three was worse than a specialist owning one, at least at a given team size. The second is also real: once an organization outgrows a room everyone fits in, someone has to do cross-team integration, and that work is genuinely full-time past a certain scale. Frameworks built for that moment, like the Scaled Agile Framework, publish entire role catalogues — Product Owner, Release Train Engineer, Solution Architect — as the honest answer to a real coordination problem at hundreds of engineers.</p>
<p>The third is the one I am actually arguing with: those same catalogues got installed at teams a tenth that size, and the seat survived long after the coordination problem it solved had shrunk back down. Nobody wrote that down as a plan. It happened one restructuring at a time, each one locally reasonable — a promotion needed a title, a title needed a scope, a scope needed a headcount line to protect it. The result reads, from where I sit, less like specialization and more like a piloting layer: a seat whose main output was status, not judgment. Its existence let the people doing the work stop feeling responsible for the parts the seat now covered. That reading is mine. No framework I am about to cite makes it for me.</p>
<p><abbr title="Matthew Skelton and Manuel Pais, authors of the 2019 book on organizing teams around cognitive load">Team Topologies</abbr> gets closest to a citable version of the mechanism, and it argues something narrower than my claim: cognitive load is finite, so a team should shed every hand-off it does not need. <abbr title="DevOps Research and Assessment, Google Cloud's research program on software delivery">DORA</abbr>'s own research backs the same instinct from the systems side — elite performers are measurably more likely to run a loosely coupled architecture, precisely because it removes cross-team dependency on the critical path. Neither one argues for merging PO and security and domain expertise into a single person. What they argue is that every hand-off has a cost, and an organization that keeps adding seats without asking what hand-off each one adds is paying that cost blind.</p>
<p>The clearest real-world precedent for actually collapsing a seat, rather than just reducing its hand-offs, is the disappearance of a dedicated "operations" title. <abbr title="Amazon's chief technology officer">Werner Vogels</abbr> described it in 2006: giving Amazon's developers the operational responsibility for what they built raised quality on both sides of the old wall, because the two halves of the judgment — build it well, run it well — had never actually needed two different people. Netflix wrote the same move up a decade later as "full-cycle developers," folding the separate SRE and test-engineering seats back into the person writing the change. Testing and security are living through the same move right now, under the name "shift left": instead of a QA seat catching what a developer missed downstream, or a security seat gatekeeping a release at the end, the skill returns to whoever is already closest to the change when it is made.</p>

