<h2>One field report, not a template</h2>
<p>On <abbr title="Fictitious codename: the real project, its products and its vendor are anonymized.">Solstice*</abbr>, I am the tech lead for the engineering side of a hospital <abbr title="Enterprise Resource Planning — the administrative backbone of a hospital group">ERP</abbr>, and I have spent enough years in this domain that I could already argue a clinical workflow with a medical director before I ever touched a backlog. The tech lead title already meant I owned the architecture judgment and the development judgment — that was never in question, and it is not what changed. What changed is the third one: the business judgment, reading what a hospital actually needs and turning it into a priority, which used to sit with a separate PO. Over the past two quarters, I stopped handing product decisions to a separate PO and started making them myself, in the same conversation where I make the technical call. The weekly workshop that used to end with someone else writing tickets from my notes now ends with the tickets written, because the person deciding what we build and the person who understands why the certified module handles retention a certain way are the same person, in the same meeting, at the same time.</p>
<p>Naming why this was possible now, and not three years ago, matters more than the fact that it happened. The relay work that used to fill a PO's week — feeding the backlog, formatting tickets, chasing status for a steering committee — is exactly what I described agents absorbing in <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="/posts/po-is-dead-judgment-is-not">an earlier post</a>. That absorption is not the argument of this post, but it is the precondition for it: it freed enough hours in a week for one person to hold product judgment and domain depth at the same time, without simply doubling that person's workload. Before agents did that packaging work, fusing the two roles meant either the product side or the technical side went shallow. Now it does not have to.</p>
<p>I want to be direct about what does not generalize from this. It worked because I already had ten years in this domain before I added the product skill on top — I was not asking a generalist to become an expert in two things at once, I was asking someone who already half-owned one of them to formally own both. "Promote your best engineer to also run the backlog" is a recipe for burnout if the second skill is not already half-built; it is a bus-factor problem if that person becomes the only one who can translate domain nuance into a technical constraint. Past roughly fifteen to twenty engineers, or more than one product surface, I would expect this exact shape to break — the fusion becomes two full-time jobs wearing one badge, not one job with better ergonomics. What generalizes is not "make your tech lead do this." It is: stop assuming a skill needs its own seat before checking whether it already lives next to the accountability you need.</p>

<h2>This sharpens something I only got half right</h2>
<p>I wrote, two posts ago, that <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="/posts/po-is-dead-judgment-is-not">product judgment survives and diffuses across roles</a> — the engineer making micro-decisions hourly, the PM steering by delivery numbers, the PO feeding context through the workshop. That is still true at the scale that piece was describing: a multi-role team where the wall between product and engineering is dissolving from both sides at once. What I did not say clearly enough there is that diffusion has a shape, and the shape depends on how small the team gets. Spread thin enough — a squad small enough for one person to already hold the full context — diffusion does not stay spread. It collapses to one name. That is not a failure of the earlier argument. It is what the earlier argument looks like at the point where there is only one person left to diffuse to.</p>
<p>I am naming this directly because a reader of the series will notice the shift before I do it for them. The earlier post argued judgment should not consolidate into one role without a named answer to who signs and how juniors learn it. Both answers get harder, not easier, when the role is one person instead of four. I do not think that makes the fusion wrong. I think it makes the honest section below non-negotiable instead of optional.</p>
<blockquote><p>Judgment did not move to a title. It moved to whoever was already accountable and had room left in the week.</p></blockquote>

